      "writtenOn": "2020-10-20T23:32:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m not worried about the duration of the operation; if the contents of the vector are std::move() assigned to the internal copy, even the strings shouldn\u0027t need to be copied.\n\nMy concern was about possible (but not necessarily _actual_) deadlock risks - if a locking mechanism is used between the callback thread and the wake reason processing thread - _if_ the SCS write to /sys/power/state returns before all userspace tasks are unfrozen.  (I don\u0027t know if it does, so it might not even be a real risk.)\n\nBut the idea of using a wakelock hadn\u0027t occurred to me at all.  If that seems like an acceptable solution, I do think it would address the issue without having to introduce synchronization between those two threads.  But I think it would lead to one thread (executing the callback) acquiring the wake lock and trusting another thread (processing the wakeup reasons) to release it, which seems less than ideal.",
